  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery evidence using the prior conservative practical benchmark of about 0.1 absolute dependency-score difference as scientifically important. Because dependency scores are negative continuous variables and percentage relative changes are not well defined, I continued to use this absolute benchmark rather than a literal relative 10% threshold. All returned estimates are already signed to the registered direction: for direction -1, positive means stronger dependency; for direction +1, positive means weaker dependency.\n\nThe formal MTAP interaction for the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2 association, H94/R80, was small and uncertain: signed +0.056 (95% -0.046 to +0.158). It does not support the registered direction that the TP53-intact CDKN2A-MDM2 effect is meaningfully modified by MTAP loss. This is consistent with the earlier MTAP decomposition: MTAP alone was mostly null for MDM2 (H80, H84-H86), and the MTAP-intact CDKN2A stratum still had a directionally positive but imprecise effect (H79). I therefore reject and close H94. This does not prove there is no MTAP interaction; it means the current evidence does not justify treating MTAP status as a meaningful modifier of H77.\n\nH95/R81 is the only potentially important new result. Among TP53-loss cells, CDKN2A loss was associated with stronger MDM2 dependency: signed +0.111 (95% +0.035 to +0.186), with adequate cell sizes (81 exposed, 325 comparator). The point estimate is around the importance benchmark and the interval excludes zero, but the lower confidence bound is below 0.1 and this arose after many secondary context probes. It is therefore not strong enough to accept without independent confirmation. This result is scientifically relevant because it challenges a narrow interpretation of H77 as a TP53-intact-only association, but it does not overturn H77 itself: H77 remains accepted for TP53-intact cells based on its discovery and validation evidence. H95 should be treated as an ambiguous possible broader CDKN2A-MDM2 association, and I requested validation for it because it is the best new candidate for a moderate secondary axis rather than a small null.\n\nH96/R82 tested whether the TP53-intact CDKN2A-MDM2 association is modified by lineage subtype. The interaction was signed -0.058 (95% -0.148 to +0.031), opposite to the registered direction and below the importance benchmark. There is no evidence that adenocarcinoma status meaningfully modifies the accepted TP53-intact effect, so H96 is rejected and closed. H97/R83 tested specificity by asking whether CDKN2A loss also predicts weaker USP7 dependency in TP53-intact cells. The estimate was essentially null: signed -0.003 (95% -0.025 to +0.019). This supports the interpretation that CDKN2A loss is not a broad p53-axis marker with generic USP7 effects, and H97 is rejected and closed.\n\nThe context probes for the accepted large axes were negative. H98/R84 found no evidence that higher aneuploidy modifies the MSI-high association with stronger WRN dependency: signed +0.022 (95% -0.037 to +0.081). H99/R85 found no evidence that PTEN loss modifies the TP53-loss association with weaker USP7 dependency: signed +0.064 (95% -0.145 to +0.273). Both are rejected and closed. These nulls are reassuring for the robustness of H10 and H11 across these contexts, but they should not be interpreted as equivalence tests.\n\nThe new mechanistic probes were mostly null or inconsistent. TP53 loss did not support weaker RPL11 dependency (H100/R86: +0.004; 95% -0.016 to +0.024) or stronger WRN dependency (H101/R87: -0.005; 95% -0.034 to +0.023). EGFR amplification did not support stronger STAT3 dependency (H102/R88: -0.034; 95% -0.068 to +0.001), and ERBB2 amplification did not support stronger STAT3 or RIT1 dependency at a meaningful size (H103/R89: +0.022; 95% -0.015 to +0.059; H104/R90: -0.006; 95% -0.035 to +0.023). MYC amplification in TP53-intact cells also did not support stronger MDM2 dependency (H105/R91: +0.008; 95% -0.023 to +0.040). All are rejected and closed as not supporting the registered associations.\n\nTo allocate follow-up efficiently, I registered one proposal for a broader CDKN2A-MDM2 test without the TP53-loss restriction. This will synthesize the current pattern if H95 is validated: CDKN2A loss may mark stronger MDM2 dependency in both TP53 contexts, but the TP53-intact claim H77 remains the currently accepted axis. If H95 fails validation, the TP53-intact interpretation of H77 remains intact and the broader claim should be interpreted cautiously.",
  "research_notes": "Round-7 appraise update. New discovery context/specificity probes: (1) H94 MTAP interaction for CDKN2A-MDM2 null/small, reject. (2) H95 CDKN2A loss in TP53-loss cells showed positive MDM2 effect +0.111 (95% +0.035 to +0.186); unresolved/active and validation requested because it may indicate broader CDKN2A-MDM2 effect but is borderline and multiple-test sensitive. If validated above benchmark, accept a TP53-loss CDKN2A-MDM2 association; if not, reject/close and keep H77 as accepted TP53-intact association. (3) H96 lineage interaction for TP53-intact CDKN2A-MDM2 reject; no subtype modification support. (4) H97 USP7 specificity control for CDKN2A in TP53-intact null, reject; supports MDM2-specific rather than generic p53-axis marker. (5) H98 aneuploidy interaction for MSI-WRN null, reject; supports robustness of accepted MSI-WRN axis. (6) H99 PTEN interaction for TP53-USP7 null, reject; supports robustness of accepted USP7 axis. (7) New probes H100-H105 all null or below benchmark: TP53->RPL11, TP53->WRN, EGFR amp->STAT3, ERBB2 amp->STAT3, ERBB2 amp->RIT1, MYC amp TP53-intact->MDM2; reject/close. New proposal registered: unconditional CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency, parent H77, motivated by R59/R78/R81, for later analysis. Validation remaining after H95 request should be reserved for possible new strong axes or ambiguous accepted-context results, not small nulls.",
